-- title:
--   The cone gap as norm times the sine of the angular deficit
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ with $\lVert v\rVert = 1$, and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$. For $h \in E$ let $\angle(v,h) \in [0,\pi]$ denote the unoriented angle between $v$ and $h$. Then
--
--   $$\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,h) \;=\; \lVert h\rVert \, \sin\bigl(\vartheta - \angle(v,h)\bigr),$$
--
--   where $\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,h) = \langle v,h\rangle \sin\vartheta - \lVert h - \langle v,h\rangle v\rVert \cos\vartheta$ is the signed distance from $h$ to the boundary of the half-cone of axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$.
--
--   This identity is the geometric meaning of the gap: it is the length of $h$ times the sine of how far the direction of $h$ falls inside (or outside, if negative) the angular aperture $\vartheta$, and hence exactly the distance from $h$ to the cone boundary. It is what makes the constants of the renormalization step computable, since it converts an angular hypothesis such as $\angle(v,h) \le \vartheta/2$ directly into a metric amount of room.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No restriction is placed on $\vartheta$ beyond being real. For $h = 0$ both sides vanish, so the convention Mathlib uses for the angle at the zero vector is irrelevant to the statement.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ConeGap.lean#L229-L238

